\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Unless a section says otherwise, ${\mathbb{N}}=\{1,2,\ldots\}$,
${\mathbb{Z}}$ is the ring of integers, ${\mathbb{Q}},{\mathbb{R}},{\mathbb{C}}$ are the rational, real, and complex
fields, $[N]=\{1,\ldots,\lfloor N\rfloor\}$, and $\log$ is the natural
logarithm. For real functions of a parameter tending to infinity,
$f\ll g$ and $f=O(g)$ mean $|f|\leq Cg$ eventually for some fixed $C$;
$f\gg g$ reverses this comparison; $f=o(g)$ means $f/g\to0$;
$f\sim g$ means $f/g\to1$; and $f\asymp g$ means both order bounds.
A subscript on $O$ or $\ll$ specifies allowed constant dependence.
The expression $n^{o(1)}$ in an upper bound means growth smaller than
$n^\epsilon$ for each fixed $\epsilon>0$. Local definitions govern any
exception, finite-field convention, or use of the same symbol for another
object. An asymptotic claim is not automatically an effective algorithm.



The increasing ordering makes the next choice the least integer greater than the previous term that is not the sum of a nonempty consecutive block of earlier terms. The density question concerns the set of values, with the initial seed held fixed.

For an integer set $A$, its natural density is $\lim_{X\to\infty}|A\cap[1,X]|/X$, when this limit exists. Upper and lower densities use limit superior and limit inferior. A request for density is not automatically satisfied by positive upper density alone. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]} \href{https://www.erdosproblems.com/359}{[S2]}

\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Let $a_1<a_2<\cdots$ be an infinite sequence of integers such that $a_1=n$ and $a_{i+1}$ is the least integer which is not a sum of consecutive earlier $a_j$s. What can be said about the density of this sequence?
In particular, in the case $n=1$, can one prove that $a_k/k\to \infty$ and $a_k/k^{1+c}\to 0$ for any $c>0$? \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
What is the density of the sequence that greedily avoids sums of earlier consecutive terms, especially when it begins with one?

